WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.180
Potassium has far more conductance than sodium.

2
00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:09.580
So why doesn't the cell rest right at potassium's mark?

3
00:00:10.533 --> 00:00:11.673
Back to the scale.

4
00:00:12.653 --> 00:00:13.873
Potassium's rope is thick.

5
00:00:14.973 --> 00:00:15.973
Sodium's rope is thin.

6
00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:20.233
The marker slides until the two pulls are equal and opposite.

7
00:00:21.153 --> 00:00:22.613
That spot is the balance point.

8
00:00:23.583 --> 00:00:25.763
Which end does the marker settle nearer?

9
00:00:26.603 --> 00:00:27.903
And roughly how far along?

10
00:00:33.883 --> 00:00:38.923
Nearer potassium. The pulls are equal, so the thin rope must be stretched farther.

11
00:00:40.017 --> 00:00:43.377
Potassium's rope is about five and a half times stronger.

12
00:00:43.937 --> 00:00:47.357
So the marker sits about five and a half times closer to its mark.

13
00:00:48.317 --> 00:00:51.597
That puts rest at about minus −71 mV.

14
00:00:52.277 --> 00:00:55.517
And the two gaps are the sizes of the driving forces from before.

15
00:00:56.567 --> 00:00:58.467
The algebra says the same thing.

16
00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:02.167
Set the currents to add up to zero, and solve for the voltage.

17
00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:07.950
The resting potential comes out as an average of the equilibrium potentials.

18
00:01:08.783 --> 00:01:11.183
Each one is weighted by its conductance.

19
00:01:11.963 --> 00:01:15.083
More ions just add more terms, above and below the line.

20
00:01:16.217 --> 00:01:19.417
Do not shorten this to a rule about the biggest conductance.

21
00:01:20.117 --> 00:01:22.977
With three or more conductances, that shortcut can fail.

22
00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:25.577
Three consequences follow.

23
00:01:26.657 --> 00:01:30.677
Open more of one ion's channels, and the balance point slides toward that ion's

24
00:01:30.677 --> 00:01:31.677
mark.

25
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:33.700
Close some, and it slides away.

26
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.260
Scale every leak up together, and the passive balance point stays put.

27
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:44.680
But something else changes, as later chapters show.

28
00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:49.130
Double the sodium conductance, and rest moves toward sodium.

29
00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:53.270
But only partway, because potassium's rope is still stronger.

30
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:58.353
Keep one number from this cell: its total leak, 10 nS.

31
00:01:59.153 --> 00:02:00.753
It comes back in the next chapter.

32
00:02:01.767 --> 00:02:05.807
Often you know concentrations and permeabilities, not conductances.

33
00:02:06.807 --> 00:02:09.567
Then you calculate with the Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation.

34
00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:14.050
It writes the same zero net current condition in those terms.

35
00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:17.470
Each ion counts in proportion to its permeability.

36
00:02:18.533 --> 00:02:22.973
For our cell, it gives the same rest, about minus −71 mV.

37
00:02:23.833 --> 00:02:27.713
Permeability and conductance are different measures of the same channels.

38
00:02:28.766 --> 00:02:30.946
Permeability describes the pathway.

39
00:02:31.926 --> 00:02:36.626
Conductance here is the current divided by its driving force, the gap between the

40
00:02:36.626 --> 00:02:39.006
voltage and the ion's equilibrium potential.

41
00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:42.966
It also depends on the ions on each side, and the voltage.

42
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:45.800
So reason with the balance point.

43
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:51.320
Calculate with the Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation when you have concentrations and

44
00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:52.320
permeabilities.

45
00:02:53.033 --> 00:02:57.633
One more piece. Left alone, these leaks would run the gradients down.

46
00:02:58.666 --> 00:03:01.486
The sodium-potassium pump puts the ions back.

47
00:03:02.386 --> 00:03:05.826
Three sodium go out and two potassium come in, for each ATP.

48
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.760
So rest is a steady state, kept up by the pump.

49
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:11.800
It is not an equilibrium.

50
00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:16.000
The pump's own current can also shift rest a little more negative.

51
00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.360
In many cells that shift is small, but it varies.

52
00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:25.653
With no current injected, and the pump's own current set aside, rest is where the

53
00:03:25.653 --> 00:03:29.873
leaks balance: the conductance-weighted average of the reversal potentials.
